<p><em>Theodore Roosevelt Roughrider</em> portrays North Dakota's most famous adopted son as colonel of the regiment he commanded during the Spanish-American War. Sculptor {% person "alexander-phimister-proctor" %}Alexander Phimister Proctor{% endperson %} was born in Canada and raised in Colorado. A contemporary of {% person "frederic-remington" %}Frederic Remington{% endperson %} and {% person "charles-m-russell" %}Charles Russell{% endperson %}, Proctor dedicated his life to monumental sculpture. His other equestrian works include statues of General {% person "william-t-sherman" %}William T. Sherman{% endperson %} in New York's Central Park and General {% person "robert-e-lee" %}Robert E. Lee{% endperson %} in Dallas, as well as Denver's <em>Bronco Buster</em>.</p>

<p>Proctor's original Roosevelt statue was erected in Portland, Oregon, and became the subject of the Metropolitan Museum of Art's first educational film about a sculptor's work. Proctor met {% person "theodore-roosevelt" %}Theodore Roosevelt{% endperson %} at the 1893 World's Fair, and Roosevelt later commissioned him to create several sculptures for the White House.</p>

<p>According to the Inventory of American Sculpture, Mandan's sculpture is a smaller version of the one commissioned by {% person "henry-waldo-coe" %}Dr. Henry Waldo Coe{% endperson %} for Portland. Proctor explained that he wanted to portray Roosevelt's fearlessness, courage, restrained energy, dignity, and reserve rather than simply his popular reputation for constant action.</p>

<p>Coe met Roosevelt while living in Dakota Territory, and they formed a lasting friendship. Coe represented Mandan residents in the last territorial legislature in 1885 and served as the city's eighth mayor in 1888. He and his wife moved to Portland in 1891. After commissioning the original statue for Portland, he gave the smaller version to Mandan.</p>

<p>The Roosevelt family approved the design. {% person "edith-roosevelt" %}Edith Roosevelt{% endperson %} and {% person "kermit-roosevelt" %}Kermit Roosevelt{% endperson %} found the uniform Roosevelt wore at San Juan Hill in family trunks and provided it to Proctor as a reference. Roosevelt's widow, all five surviving children, and their spouses attended the dedication.</p>

