<p>The statue was cast in 1922 at Roman Bronze Works in New York and dedicated on July 2, 1924. The bronze measures 56 by 56 by 20 inches. Its stone base, prepared by Mandan master stonecutter {% person "hynek-rybnicek" %}Hynek Rybnicek{% endperson %}, measures 57 by 72 by 56 inches.</p>

<p>After the unveiling, the <em>Mandan Daily Pioneer</em> described a crowd that included hundreds of cowboys, approximately 500 Native Americans, 600 American Legion members attending the state convention, schoolchildren, pioneers, businesspeople in roundup clothing, and service members in uniform. A message from President {% person "calvin-coolidge" %}Coolidge{% endperson %} was also read.</p>

<p>A full-size replica was cast in bronze in 1923 by the Henry Bonnard Foundry of New York. Mounted on a California light-gray Raymond granite pedestal, it is displayed in Minot. During his lifetime, Coe refused requests to copy the statue for Fairmount Park in Philadelphia and Central Park in New York. A 12-inch cast remained in the State Historical Society of North Dakota archives for more than seven decades before being transferred to the Proctor Museum in Wyoming in 2007.</p>

